  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 318 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nA cold front will cross the forecast area Monday evening \nbringing an increased threat for thunderstorms across the area. \nINVOF the front, storm coverage will be initially isolated with \nmore scattered coverage into the evening and nighttime hours. \nWarm mid level temperatures and decent capping will limit \nthunderstorm coverage initially. By evening, cooler mid level \ntemperatures will arrive as a mid level shortwave approaches \nnorthern Nebraska. Post frontal precipitation seems like a \nbetter bet ATTM with the arrival of this mid level support. With\nthis in mind, have trended the highest pops into Monday evening\nand overnight hours. As for the severe threat Monday evening. \nThere is a narrow corridor of 1500 to 2500 J/KG CAPE late Monday\nafternoon into mid evening Monday. Given the degree of capping \npresent across the area, the severe threat appears highly \nconditional ATTM. If storms do initiate in the late \nafternoon/early evening time frame, the main threat will be hail\nand damaging winds. Overnight, the front will drop south of the\narea, stalling over Kansas. Easterly and northeasterly winds, \nalong with expected cloud cover, will limit highs to the 70s on \nTuesday and Wednesday. North of the front, a mid level trough \nwill approach and track across the northern plains Tuesday \nnight, leading to increased mid level frontogenesis across the \nsouthern 2/3rds of the forecast area. With support from the \ndeterministic EC soln the latest NBM has a broad swath of 60 to \n70% chances of QPF>0.10 inches Tuesday night into Wednesday \nmorning across the southern half of the forecast area. This is \nalso supported to a lesser degree with the latest NAM12 and GFS \nsolns tonight. That being said, low end likely pops in the \nsouthern half of the FA Tuesday night into Wednesday morning \nseems reasonable ATTM. Beyond Wednesday, mid level hts will \nbegin to slowly increase as ridging builds from the SW CONUS \nnortheast onto the central and southern plains. Precipitation \nchances will be spotty at best and the threat for organized \nmeasurable precipitation appears low. Highs will rebound into \nthe 80s for Thursday with readings reaching into the 90s for \nFriday and Saturday. Even warmer temperatures may be on the \nhorizon the following week as the latest deterministic GFS is \nindicating H85 temps approaching the mid 30sC across the area \nSunday into Monday (Aug 9-10).",
